**Define the tool twice: as a Python function your application can execute, and as a JSON schema the LLM can read.** Then write a dispatcher that converts the model’s tool-call arguments into a Python function invocation.

That is the key distinction in [*Agents Zero to Hero #1*](https://nik-hil.hashnode.dev/agents-zero-to-hero-1-building-an-ai-agent-from-scratch-with-tool-calling): **the model decides what to request; your application—the “harness”—executes it.** A tool schema alone does not run any code.

## 1. Define a function, schema, and registry

The article exposes `execute_code` and `finish`. Here is the same pattern using a safer, narrowly scoped `add` tool:

```python
def add(a: float, b: float) -> dict:
    # Type hints alone do not enforce types at runtime.
    if type(a) not in (int, float) or type(b) not in (int, float):
        raise ValueError("a and b must be numbers")
    return {"result": a + b}


TOOLS = {"add": add}

TOOL_SCHEMAS = [
    {
        "type": "function",
        "function": {
            "name": "add",
            "description": "Add two numbers.",
            "strict": True,
            "parameters": {
                "type": "object",
                "properties": {
                    "a": {"type": "number"},
                    "b": {"type": "number"},
                },
                "required": ["a", "b"],
                "additionalProperties": False,
            },
        },
    }
]
```

The **schema** tells the model the tool’s name, purpose, and accepted arguments. The **registry** maps that name to an actual callable in your process. This follows the schema-and-tool-map approach described in the [article](https://nik-hil.hashnode.dev/agents-zero-to-hero-1-building-an-ai-agent-from-scratch-with-tool-calling).

